  <h1>Low-Latency C++ Best Practices</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: Lesson 0009 cleaned up
    <em>one</em> hot loop. Real HFT C++ is multi-threaded — a feed thread,
    a strategy thread, a logger thread — communicating through lock-free rings,
    pinned to isolated cores, compiled with non-default flags. This is the
    layer that turns a fast loop into a production system.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 ARCHITECTURE ===================== -->
  <h2>1. The production architecture: three threads, two rings</h2>

  <p>
    Every serious HFT system in C++ has the same shape, regardless of strategy.
    The hot path is split across dedicated threads, each doing one thing, each
    pinned to one core, talking through lock-free queues.
  </p>

  <div class="arch">
    <div class="arch-row">
      <div class="arch-box">
        <strong>Feed thread</strong>
        Reads market data from the NIC (kernel-bypass or FPGA), parses ticks, pushes onto a ring.
      </div>
      <div class="arch-arrow">─SPSC─►</div>
      <div class="arch-box">
        <strong>Strategy thread</strong>
        Drains ticks from the ring, runs <code>MeanReversion::onTick</code>, decides BUY/HOLD/SELL.
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="arch-row">
      <div class="arch-box" style="grid-column: 1 / span 2;">
        <strong>Strategy thread</strong> (continued)
        Emits orders directly to the NIC; pushes trade records onto a log ring.
      </div>
      <div class="arch-arrow">─SPSC─►</div>
      <div class="arch-box">
        <strong>Logger thread</strong>
        Tails the log ring, writes to disk / Kafka / stderr. Not latency-critical.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Why this shape and not "one big main loop"?</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Isolation.</strong> The logger can stall on disk I/O without slowing the strategy.</li>
    <li><strong>Pinnability.</strong> Each thread gets its own dedicated CPU core; the kernel can't context-switch them.</li>
    <li><strong>Specialization.</strong> Each thread can be optimized for its own workload (busy-poll the NIC; batch-flush the disk).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The rings are the only shared state, and they are <em>lock-free</em>. That's the central object of this lesson.</p>

  <!-- ===================== §2 SPSC RING ===================== -->
  <h2>2. The SPSC ring buffer — done right</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>SPSC</strong> = single-producer, single-consumer. The simplest and
    fastest lock-free queue. One thread always pushes; one thread always pops.
    No other configuration. (For multi-producer or multi-consumer you need MPSC
    or MPMC — much more expensive and worth avoiding when you can.)
  </p>

  <p>A correct SPSC has four ingredients that beginners almost always get wrong:</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>1. Power-of-two capacity</h4>
      <p>
        Replace modulo with bitwise-and: <code>idx & (N - 1)</code> instead of
        <code>idx % N</code>. One instruction instead of an integer division.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>2. Acquire/release ordering, not seq_cst</h4>
      <p>
        <code>std::atomic</code>'s default is <code>memory_order_seq_cst</code> — a
        full barrier on every access, ~10× slower than necessary. The producer
        publishes with <code>release</code>; the consumer reads with
        <code>acquire</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>3. Cache-line padding on head/tail</h4>
      <p>
        If the producer's write index and the consumer's read index share a
        cache line, every push ping-pongs that line between cores
        (false sharing). Force them onto different lines with
        <code>alignas(64)</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>4. Storage by value, not pointer</h4>
      <p>
        The ring holds the records inline (<code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code>),
        not pointers to heap-allocated objects. Otherwise you've put the
        allocator back on the hot path.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Reference implementation</h3>

<pre><span class="kw">template</span>&lt;<span class="kw">typename</span> T, std::<span class="ty">size_t</span> N&gt;
<span class="kw">class</span> SpscRing {
    <span class="kw">static_assert</span>((N &amp; (N - <span class="num">1</span>)) == <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="str">"N must be power of 2"</span>);
    <span class="kw">static constexpr</span> <span class="ty">size_t</span> MASK = N - <span class="num">1</span>;
    <span class="kw">static constexpr</span> <span class="ty">size_t</span> CACHELINE = <span class="num">64</span>;

    std::<span class="ty">array</span>&lt;T, N&gt; data_{};

    <span class="com">// Each index sits on its own cache line — no false sharing.</span>
    <span class="kw">alignas</span>(CACHELINE) std::atomic&lt;std::<span class="ty">size_t</span>&gt; head_{<span class="num">0</span>};   <span class="com">// producer writes</span>
    <span class="kw">alignas</span>(CACHELINE) std::atomic&lt;std::<span class="ty">size_t</span>&gt; tail_{<span class="num">0</span>};   <span class="com">// consumer reads</span>

<span class="kw">public</span>:
    <span class="com">// Producer-only.  Returns false if full.</span>
    <span class="ty">bool</span> <span class="fn">try_push</span>(<span class="kw">const</span> T&amp; v) <span class="kw">noexcept</span> {
        <span class="kw">const auto</span> h = head_.<span class="fn">load</span>(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        <span class="kw">const auto</span> t = tail_.<span class="fn">load</span>(std::memory_order_acquire);
        <span class="kw">if</span> (h - t == N) <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">false</span>;        <span class="com">// full</span>
        data_[h &amp; MASK] = v;                       <span class="com">// store payload</span>
        head_.<span class="fn">store</span>(h + <span class="num">1</span>, std::memory_order_release);  <span class="com">// publish</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">true</span>;
    }

    <span class="com">// Consumer-only.  Returns false if empty.</span>
    <span class="ty">bool</span> <span class="fn">try_pop</span>(T&amp; out) <span class="kw">noexcept</span> {
        <span class="kw">const auto</span> t = tail_.<span class="fn">load</span>(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        <span class="kw">const auto</span> h = head_.<span class="fn">load</span>(std::memory_order_acquire);
        <span class="kw">if</span> (h == t) <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">false</span>;             <span class="com">// empty</span>
        out = data_[t &amp; MASK];
        tail_.<span class="fn">store</span>(t + <span class="num">1</span>, std::memory_order_release);
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">true</span>;
    }
};
</pre>

  <h3>What every memory_order line is doing</h3>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Line</th><th>Order</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>head_.load(relaxed)</code> in push</td>
      <td>relaxed</td>
      <td>The producer is the only writer of <code>head_</code> — it owns the value, doesn't need synchronization to read its own write.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>tail_.load(acquire)</code> in push</td>
      <td>acquire</td>
      <td>Sees the latest tail published by the consumer. Acquire ensures any payload reads the consumer did before publishing are visible — actually unused here but cheap.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>head_.store(release)</code> in push</td>
      <td>release</td>
      <td><strong>The publish.</strong> Guarantees the payload write to <code>data_[h &amp; MASK]</code> is visible to any thread that does an acquire-load of <code>head_</code>. Without release, the consumer could see <code>head_</code> advance while still reading stale memory.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>head_.load(acquire)</code> in pop</td>
      <td>acquire</td>
      <td>Pairs with the producer's release. After this load returns a new value, the consumer is guaranteed to see the payload that was written before it.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>The seq_cst tax.</strong> If you'd written <code>std::atomic&lt;size_t&gt;</code>
    and used the default operators (<code>head_++</code>, <code>head_ = h+1</code>),
    you'd silently get <code>memory_order_seq_cst</code>, which inserts a full
    fence (<code>MFENCE</code> on x86, ~5–10 ns) on every operation. Industry
    benchmarks show 10×+ speedups by dropping to release/acquire. Always write
    the orders explicitly.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §3 CPU ISOLATION ===================== -->
  <h2>3. CPU isolation, thread pinning, and the kernel scheduler</h2>

  <p>
    A fast hot loop is worthless if Linux can preempt it 10,000 times a second
    to run cron jobs or background kernel work. HFT systems carve out cores
    that the kernel won't touch and pin one thread to each.
  </p>

  <h3>Kernel boot parameters (set in <code>/etc/default/grub</code>)</h3>
<pre>GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX=<span class="str">"isolcpus=4-7 nohz_full=4-7 rcu_nocbs=4-7"</span></pre>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>isolcpus=4-7</code></td><td>Tells the scheduler not to place general tasks on cores 4–7. Only threads that explicitly pin to those cores will run there.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>nohz_full=4-7</code></td><td>Turns off the periodic 1 kHz timer tick on those cores when only one task is runnable. No timer interrupts = no jitter.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>rcu_nocbs=4-7</code></td><td>Offloads RCU (read-copy-update) callbacks from those cores. Removes another source of kernel work.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Pinning a thread to a core in C++</h3>
<pre>#<span class="kw">include</span> &lt;pthread.h&gt;

<span class="fn">void</span> <span class="fn">pinThread</span>(std::thread&amp; t, <span class="ty">int</span> core_id) {
    cpu_set_t cpus;
    <span class="fn">CPU_ZERO</span>(&amp;cpus);
    <span class="fn">CPU_SET</span>(core_id, &amp;cpus);
    <span class="fn">pthread_setaffinity_np</span>(t.<span class="fn">native_handle</span>(), <span class="kw">sizeof</span>(cpus), &amp;cpus);
}

<span class="com">// Wire the three threads onto isolated cores:</span>
std::thread feed_thread{<span class="fn">feed_loop</span>, &amp;tick_ring};       <span class="fn">pinThread</span>(feed_thread, <span class="num">4</span>);
std::thread strat_thread{<span class="fn">strategy_loop</span>, &amp;tick_ring, &amp;log_ring}; <span class="fn">pinThread</span>(strat_thread, <span class="num">5</span>);
std::thread log_thread{<span class="fn">log_loop</span>, &amp;log_ring};         <span class="fn">pinThread</span>(log_thread, <span class="num">6</span>);
</pre>

  <p>
    The strategy thread now runs <em>uninterrupted</em> on core 5: no scheduler
    preemption, no timer interrupts, no migration to a different cache. Combined
    with the lock-free ring, the per-tick path is fully deterministic.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Bonus tweaks the experienced teams add:</strong>
    <code>SCHED_FIFO</code> with a high priority (real-time scheduler class),
    <code>mlockall(MCL_CURRENT | MCL_FUTURE)</code> to prevent paging,
    transparent-huge-pages disabled, hyperthreading off on the
    strategy core (so it owns the L1).
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §4 BUILD DISCIPLINE ===================== -->
  <h2>4. Build &amp; compiler discipline — the flags that matter</h2>

  <p>
    Default compiler settings leave a lot of latency on the table. HFT C++
    is typically built with:
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Flag</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>-O3</code></td><td>Aggressive optimization: inlining, loop unrolling, auto-vectorization.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>-march=native</code></td><td>Uses the full instruction set of the build machine (AVX2, AVX-512, FMA). Critical for SIMD wins.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>-flto</code></td><td>Link-Time Optimization — lets the compiler inline across translation units and dead-code-eliminate globally.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>-fno-exceptions</code></td><td>Removes exception scaffolding. Pair with explicit error codes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>-fno-rtti</code></td><td>Removes <code>typeid</code> / <code>dynamic_cast</code> machinery. Almost never needed in HFT code.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>-fno-plt</code></td><td>Removes one level of indirection for shared-library calls.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>-fprofile-use</code> (PGO)</td><td>Profile-Guided Optimization: build, run with representative load to gather a profile, rebuild using it. The compiler now knows which branches are hot. Real wins: 5–15% on the hot path.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>The PGO loop</h3>
<pre>$ g++ -O3 -march=native -fprofile-generate=./prof  *.cpp -o trader
$ ./trader replay.dat                    <span class="com"># run a representative workload to collect profile</span>
$ g++ -O3 -march=native -flto -fprofile-use=./prof *.cpp -o trader_pgo
</pre>

  <p>
    The PGO binary lays out hot basic blocks contiguously, predicts branches
    based on the real profile, and inlines aggressively where the data shows
    it pays. For a measurement-driven team this is free latency.
  </p>

  <h3>SIMD: the bonus you get nearly for free</h3>
  <p>
    With <code>-O3 -march=native</code>, the compiler auto-vectorizes simple
    loops. In Lesson 0009's rewritten <code>MeanReversion::onTick</code>, the
    running-sum update is already O(1), so SIMD is irrelevant there. But if
    you batch-process e.g. 1024 ticks for offline backtest replay, the inner
    loop will be auto-vectorized into AVX-2 (4 doubles/op) or AVX-512
    (8 doubles/op) without you writing a single intrinsic. Inspect the
    assembly with <code>objdump -d</code> or <a href="https://godbolt.org">godbolt</a>
    to confirm.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §5 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>5. In this project — restructuring the C++ for production</h2>

  <p>
    The repo's three C++ files don't yet have any threading. To get production-grade,
    the responsibilities split this way:
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Existing file</th><th>Hot/cold</th><th>Becomes…</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>gradient_boosting_data_fetching.cpp</code></td>
      <td>Cold (cURL → REST API)</td>
      <td>Stays on a worker thread; pushes parsed ticks onto <code>tick_ring</code>. Allocate freely.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>sample_with_gradient_boosting.cpp</code></td>
      <td>Hot</td>
      <td>Becomes the strategy loop pinned to an isolated core; drains <code>tick_ring</code>, runs <code>onTick</code> (per Lesson 0009), pushes <code>TradeRecord</code> onto <code>log_ring</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>main.cpp</code> (ONNX inference)</td>
      <td>Warm</td>
      <td>Runs on a parameter-update thread. Refreshes the strategy's weight constants every 100 ms via a single atomic publish. Hot loop reads with <code>memory_order_acquire</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>The skeleton</h3>
<pre><span class="kw">struct</span> Tick { <span class="ty">double</span> price; <span class="ty">uint64_t</span> ts_ns; };
<span class="kw">struct</span> TradeRecord { <span class="ty">double</span> price; <span class="kw">enum class</span> Side : <span class="ty">uint8_t</span> { Buy, Sell } side; <span class="ty">uint64_t</span> ts_ns; };

<span class="kw">static</span> SpscRing&lt;Tick,        <span class="num">1</span> &lt;&lt; <span class="num">14</span>&gt; tick_ring;     <span class="com">// 16384 ticks buffered</span>
<span class="kw">static</span> SpscRing&lt;TradeRecord, <span class="num">1</span> &lt;&lt; <span class="num">14</span>&gt; log_ring;      <span class="com">// 16384 trades buffered</span>

<span class="fn">void</span> <span class="fn">strategy_loop</span>() <span class="kw">noexcept</span> {
    MeanReversion&lt;<span class="num">20</span>&gt; strat;
    Tick tk;
    <span class="kw">while</span> (running.<span class="fn">load</span>(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
        <span class="kw">if</span> (!tick_ring.<span class="fn">try_pop</span>(tk)) [[<span class="kw">unlikely</span>]] {
            <span class="com">// Spin: a few PAUSE instructions, then re-check. No syscalls, no sleep.</span>
            _mm_pause();
            <span class="kw">continue</span>;
        }
        <span class="kw">auto</span> action = strat.<span class="fn">onTick</span>(tk.price);
        <span class="kw">if</span> (action != MeanReversion::Action::Hold) [[<span class="kw">unlikely</span>]] {
            <span class="com">// Send order to the NIC (kernel-bypass or FPGA path).</span>
            <span class="fn">emit_order</span>(tk.price, action);
            log_ring.<span class="fn">try_push</span>({tk.price,
                <span class="fn">action_to_side</span>(action), tk.ts_ns});
        }
    }
}
</pre>

  <p>
    What this delivers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zero allocation per tick</strong> — the rings are pre-allocated, <code>strat</code> holds its stack array.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero I/O per tick</strong> — logging is a single <code>try_push</code> of a 24-byte record.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero syscalls per tick</strong> — busy-poll the ring with <code>_mm_pause()</code>; the kernel never sees us.</li>
    <li><strong>Deterministic per-tick time</strong> on the isolated core.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Repo honesty:</strong> none of this is implemented yet — the existing
    C++ is single-threaded and synchronous, and that's the appropriate first cut.
    The point of this lesson is what the system <em>becomes</em> when you decide
    to take latency seriously, and to give you the right vocabulary to ask
    "where would this thread live?" on every line you write.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §6 CONTRASTS ===================== -->
  <h2>6. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>SPSC vs MPSC vs MPMC</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>SPSC</strong>: one producer, one consumer — fastest. <strong>MPSC</strong>:
        many producers, one consumer (e.g. logger fed by many strategy threads).
        <strong>MPMC</strong>: many of each — most expensive. HFT teams design
        the system to need only SPSC wherever possible.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>seq_cst vs release/acquire</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>seq_cst</strong>: total global order on all atomics — strongest,
        slowest, default. <strong>release/acquire</strong>: pairs publish↔read
        of a specific atomic — enough for queues. <strong>relaxed</strong>: no
        synchronization, just atomicity — for indices the thread owns.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Lock-free vs Wait-free</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Lock-free</strong>: at least one thread always makes progress.
        <strong>Wait-free</strong>: every thread completes in bounded steps.
        SPSC rings written as above are wait-free. Most MPMC queues are only
        lock-free.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Thread pinning vs Process affinity</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Thread pinning</strong> (<code>pthread_setaffinity_np</code>):
        per-thread, fine-grained. <strong>Process affinity</strong>
        (<code>taskset</code>): whole-process at launch. HFT pins each thread
        individually because each plays a different role.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Busy polling vs Sleeping</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Busy poll</strong> the ring with <code>_mm_pause()</code> —
        latency-optimal, burns a core. <strong>Sleep</strong> on a condition
        variable — energy-friendly, adds tens of µs wakeup latency. HFT busy-polls
        on the strategy thread, sleeps on the logger.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>PGO vs LTO</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>PGO</strong> (Profile-Guided Optimization): uses a real run's
        branch probabilities to lay out code. <strong>LTO</strong> (Link-Time
        Optimization): runs the optimizer across all translation units at link
        time. They are independent and stack — use both.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

