  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q1. Why does <code>SpscRing</code> use <code>alignas(64)</code> on the head and tail indices?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The atomic guarantees atomicity; alignment doesn't change that. The reason is about cache behavior, not atomicity.">Because <code>std::atomic</code> only guarantees atomicity at 64-byte alignment.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Modern x86 cache lines are 64 bytes. If head_ and tail_ shared a line, every push (producer write) would invalidate the consumer's cached copy and vice versa — the line would ping-pong between cores, costing ~10 ns per operation. alignas(64) forces them onto different lines.">To force head_ and tail_ onto different cache lines so that the producer's writes don't invalidate the consumer's cached read of the other variable (false sharing).</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="64 isn't a magic SIMD width here — that would be 32 or 64 bytes depending on AVX2/AVX-512. The reason is cache-line based, not SIMD.">To make the indices SIMD-vectorizable.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — every memory access works at any alignment for 8-byte values. The cost is cache coherence traffic, not allocator boundaries.">Because the heap allocator requires 64-byte alignment.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q2. The producer publishes a new entry by calling <code>head_.store(h + 1, std::memory_order_release)</code>. What does <code>release</code> actually guarantee?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="release is one-way: it orders prior writes before the store. It says nothing about loads happening after the store.">That any load <em>after</em> this store also sees the latest data.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Release pairs with acquire. It promises that all writes done by the producer thread BEFORE the head_.store (including writing data_[h & MASK] = v) become visible to any consumer thread that does an acquire-load of head_. Without release, the consumer could see head_ advance while still reading stale or torn payload memory.">That any writes the producer did before this store — specifically the payload write into <code>data_[h &amp; MASK]</code> — are visible to any consumer thread that acquire-loads <code>head_</code>.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — release doesn't synchronize the producer with itself. The producer is the only writer of head_; it doesn't need to synchronize against its own future operations.">That the producer's own subsequent reads of <code>head_</code> see the updated value.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="release does NOT insert a full fence on x86 — it's typically free, because x86 has a strong memory model. seq_cst is what adds the MFENCE.">That a full hardware fence (MFENCE) is inserted on x86 before the store.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
