  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q5. The strategy thread busy-polls the tick ring with <code>_mm_pause()</code> instead of sleeping on a condition variable. What's the tradeoff?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="_mm_pause() does not put the core to sleep. It's a hint to the CPU's pipeline that this is a spin-wait loop, so it should slow speculative execution and conserve power, not halt.">Busy polling is strictly worse — sleeping is faster.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Busy poll keeps the wakeup latency at near-zero (~10s of ns) because the thread is already running when the next tick arrives. The cost: that core is at 100% utilization continuously, burning ~25 W even when nothing is happening. A condition-variable sleep is energy-efficient but adds ~10–30 µs of wakeup latency every event — fatal for HFT.">Busy poll gives near-zero wakeup latency (the thread is already running) but burns one core at 100% utilization. Sleeping is energy-efficient but adds ~10–30 µs of wakeup latency per event.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Reordering by the CPU is exactly what acquire/release prevent at the relevant boundary. _mm_pause is unrelated to those guarantees.">Busy polling defeats the memory ordering — the CPU is allowed to reorder freely.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — _mm_pause is a hint, not a sleep. The thread keeps running. Energy is still consumed; the win is wakeup latency.">Busy polling lets the kernel safely sleep the thread for microseconds at a time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §8 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>8. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. You introduce the SpscRing but forget the <code>alignas(64)</code> on head_/tail_. The microbenchmark shows the queue does ~5 M ops/s with both threads on the same physical core, but drops to ~2 M ops/s when the threads are pinned to different cores. Explain.</summary>
    <p>
      Classic false sharing. When both threads share a core, they share the L1
      cache and the head_/tail_ ping-pong is invisible — same cache, no
      coherence traffic. When you pin them to different cores, every producer
      write to head_ invalidates the consumer's cached copy of the same line
      (because tail_ lives there too). The line ping-pongs between L1s via the
      L3, costing ~30 ns per round-trip. Fix: <code>alignas(64)</code> on each
      atomic so they live on separate cache lines. Counter-intuitively, the
      "more isolated" topology gets slower without the fix.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. Your team enables <code>-fno-exceptions</code> and a header-only library you depend on stops compiling because it throws <code>std::runtime_error</code> on bad input. What are your options?</summary>
    <p>
      Three workable paths. (a) <strong>Wall it off</strong>: keep
      <code>-fno-exceptions</code> on the hot-path TU and link in the
      library-using TU compiled with exceptions enabled — at the cost of
      losing some cross-module optimization. (b) <strong>Fork the dependency</strong>
      to return error codes; ugly but bounded. (c) <strong>Don't use it on
      the hot path</strong>; call it only during setup, where exceptions are
      fine. The wrong answer is "leave exceptions on globally": you give up
      the bandwidth/code-size win without solving the root problem, which is
      that this library was never intended for HFT-hot use.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. Two scenarios. (a) The logger ring fills up — the logger is slower than the strategy. (b) The tick ring fills up — the strategy is slower than the feed. What's the right policy for each?</summary>
    <p>
      (a) <strong>Logger ring full</strong>: the strategy must not block. The
      sane policy is to drop the log record and increment a "dropped_log_count"
      atomic that the logger reads later. Trades still happen; you lose
      diagnostic data, not money. (b) <strong>Tick ring full</strong>: this
      means the strategy is falling behind real-time. Different problem
      entirely. Options: drop the oldest tick (latest-is-truth), drop the
      incoming tick (most-recent-state-is-truth), or escalate to a panic
      shutdown. The choice depends on whether your strategy needs every tick
      or just the latest market state — but never block the feed, because the
      next tick is already arriving on the wire.
    </p>
  </details>

